  <div id="lessonContent">
    <h2>1. What is JavaScript?</h2>
    <p><b>JavaScript (JS)</b> is a programming language that makes a website <b>interactive and alive</b>.</p>
    <p>HTML = Structure (Skeleton)<br>CSS = Design (Beauty / Style)<br><b>JavaScript = Brain / Action (Movement)</b></p>
    <div class="note"><b>Simple Example:</b> HTML shows a button. CSS makes that button beautiful. <b>JavaScript makes that button work</b> - click it and an alert appears, a counter increases, or a form is checked.</div>
    <h2>2. LIVE DEMO - Try JavaScript Now</h2>
    <p>Click the buttons below. All of this is JavaScript:</p>
    <div class="demo-card">
      <h3 id="demoText" style="margin:0">Hello! I am a Website.</h3>
      <p id="demoCount" style="color:#64748b;margin:8px 0">You clicked 0 times</p>
      <button class="btn btn-js" onclick="changeText()">Click Me - Change Text 👆</button>
      <button class="btn btn-next" onclick="showAlert()">Show Alert 🔔</button>
      <button class="btn" onclick="resetDemo()">Reset ↺</button>
      <p style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13px;color:#64748b">Open Browser Console (F12) and see the console.log message too.</p>
    </div>
    <h2>3. Where Does JavaScript Run?</h2>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Place</th><th>Example</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Browser</td><td>Chrome, Firefox - Button click, Form check, Slider</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Website</td><td>Facebook Like, YouTube Play, Amazon Cart</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mobile App</td><td>React Native apps</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Server</td><td>Node.js - Backend (later)</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p><b>For now, we will use JS only in the Browser.</b> No installation is needed. The browser already understands JS.</p>
    <h2>4. How to Add JavaScript? - 3 Ways</h2>
    <p><b>Way 1 - Inside HTML (Best for Beginners):</b></p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">alert("Hello World");</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <p><b>Way 2 - In Head:</b> Write the &lt;script&gt; tag inside &lt;head&gt;.<br><b>Way 3 - Separate File:</b> &lt;script src="script.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt; - This is used in big projects.</p>
    <div class="note"><b>Rule:</b> Always place the &lt;script&gt; tag <b>just before &lt;/body&gt;</b>, so that HTML loads first, and then JS runs.</div>
    <h2>5. Your First 4 JavaScript Commands</h2>
    <p><b>1. alert() - Popup Message</b></p>
    <div class="code-box"><code><span class="text">alert("Welcome to My Website!");</span></code></div>
    <div class="result"><b>Result:</b> A popup will appear in the browser - "Welcome to My Website!"</div>
    <p><b>2. console.log() - For Developer (F12)</b></p>
    <div class="code-box"><code><span class="text">console.log("Hello - This is for developer");</span></code></div>
    <p>The user cannot see this. The developer opens F12 &gt; Console to see it. It is used for debugging.</p>
    <p><b>3. document.write() - Write on Page</b></p>
    <div class="code-box"><code><span class="text">document.write("Hello World");</span></code></div>
    <p><b>4. innerHTML - Change Text on Page (Most Important)</b></p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;h1</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"myText"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Hello</span><span class="tag">&lt;/h1&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"change()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Click Me</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="text">function change() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">document.getElementById("myText").innerHTML = "You Clicked Me!";</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <p><b>Understand this:</b> <code style="background:#e2e8f0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:4px">getElementById("myText")</code> = Find the element whose id is myText.<br><code style="background:#e2e8f0;padding:2px 6px;border-radius:4px">innerHTML = "..."</code> = Change the text inside that element.</p>
    <h2>6. onclick - Button + JavaScript</h2>
    <p><b>onclick</b> means - "When clicked, run this function".</p>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"sayHello()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Click Me</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="text">function sayHello() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">alert("Hello! You clicked the button.");</span><br>
<span class="text">}</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>7. Complete Example - Copy and Try in Playground</h2>
    <div class="code-box"><code>
<span class="tag">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;html&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;body&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;h1</span> <span class="attr">id</span>=<span class="val">"title"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">My First JS Website</span><span class="tag">&lt;/h1&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"changeText()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Change Text</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;button</span> <span class="attr">onclick</span>=<span class="val">"showAlert()"</span><span class="tag">&gt;</span><span class="text">Show Alert</span><span class="tag">&lt;/button&gt;</span><br><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;script&gt;</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function changeText() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">document.getElementById("title").innerHTML = "JavaScript is Amazing!";</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">function showAlert() {</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">alert("Hello! You are learning JavaScript.");</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text">}</span><br>
&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="tag">&lt;/script&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/body&gt;</span><br>
<span class="tag">&lt;/html&gt;</span>
    </code></div>
    <h2>8. Common Mistakes - Beginners</h2>
    <p>1. <b>Spelling:</b> <code>alert</code> is correct, <code>alret</code> is wrong - JS is very strict about spelling.<br>2. <b>Quotes:</b> <code>"Hello"</code> or <code>'Hello'</code> - both work, but opening and closing quotes must be the same.<br>3. <b>Semicolon:</b> Put <code>;</code> at the end of every line.<br>4. <b>Capital Letter:</b> In <code>getElementById</code>, B, I, D are capital - <code>getelementbyid</code> will not work.<br>5. <b>id Must Match:</b> If HTML has <code>id="title"</code>, then JS must also use <code>"title"</code>.</p>
    <h2>9. Homework</h2>
    <p>In Playground, build this:</p>
    <p>1. One <b>h1</b> - "Hello, I am [Your Name]"<br>2. One button - "Change Name" - on click, the name should change<br>3. One button - "Show Alert" - on click, an alert should show "Welcome!"<br>4. Write <b>console.log("I am learning JS")</b> and see it in F12 Console</p>
    <div class="box"><b>Remember:</b> HTML = Structure | CSS = Design | JS = Action | alert() = Popup | console.log() = Developer message | innerHTML = Change text on page | onclick = On click, run function | getElementById = Find element by id | &lt;script&gt; tag just before &lt;/body&gt;</div>
    <p><b>Next Step:</b> <a href="/lesson22">Lesson 22 - Variables</a> - Learn how to store data - let, const, var.</p>
    <a id="playgroundLink" class="btn" href="/playground">▶ Try it Yourself - Playground</a><br><br>
